  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 106 PM CDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nMid day temperatures took a nice jump into the middle 60s this past\nhour, on what has been a clear and somewhat breezy fall day across\nthe forecast area. While the visible satellite is clear, water vapor\nshows a shortwave moving over the state this hour, likely assisting\nwith the increase in winds and the jump in temperatures as it \npasses. Radar also indicating another day of active returns around \nwater bodies as the fall migration continues. \n\nThe contiguous US remains under the influence of an omega block type\npattern, with both eastern and western trofs that wane but are again \nreinforced over time. The overall trend in the forecast is a warming \none, with the NBM tightly clustered around highs and lows that rise a\nfew degrees each day, with highs in the upper 70s to near 80 by late\nweek. Worth noting that the NBM has been on the cool side with the \nhigh temperatures, so went on the higher side of guidance in the \nshort term. Some increase in mid level moisture due to shortwave \ntrofs moving into the plains Monday and Tuesday afternoons have some \nguidance giving a low chance for a shower, but the low level moisture\nappears to be enough to evaporate most attempts. Better chances may \ncome over the weekend as the western trof finally advances eastward \ninto the Central US, but the continued tropical activity over western\nMexico and its influences in the position and strength of that trof \nleave uncertainty to strength and timing of what can develop. \n\nNo freezing temperatures in the forecast this week, with overnight\nlows rising back into the 50s by Wednesday morning. The warm and\nbreezy conditions could warrant some fire weather concerns, however\nwith the lack so far of a widespread hard freeze, vegetation should\ncontinue to hold moisture to help mitigate widespread concerns.",
